WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.500
Many shell scripts run inside automation platforms rather than on a single administrator's terminal.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:20.840
CI/CD jobs, containers, deployment agents, configuration-management tasks, remote sessions, and cloud automation platforms all change how secrets are stored, injected, inherited, logged, and cleaned up.

3
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:31.340
A pattern that is acceptable for a tightly controlled local script may be risky when it runs in a pipeline that stores logs, artifacts, caches, and job metadata.

4
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.700
CI/CD secret masking is useful, but it is not a full security boundary.

5
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:49.560
Masking usually works for exact values in logs, and it may not catch derived values, transformed strings, multiline values, encoded forms, partial values, or secrets passed into tools that generate their own output.

6
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:56.540
Untrusted jobs, pull request workflows, broad variable scopes, and shared runners can also change the risk picture.

7
00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:06.240
Secrets should be scoped to the minimum project, job, branch, environment, role, or runner that needs them.

8
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.480
Containers introduce another common mistake: mixing build-time and runtime secrets.

9
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:20.220
A secret used while building an image may be captured in an image layer, cache, build log, package configuration, or artifact.

10
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.360
Runtime secrets should be injected when the container runs, not baked into the image.

11
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:33.300
When build-time secrets are unavoidable, use platform-supported secret mechanisms and verify that the value is not preserved in the final artifact.

12
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.620
Remote automation adds its own exposure paths.

13
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.120
Scripts, variables, and logs may be copied across hosts.

14
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.180
Temporary files may remain on target systems.

15
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.200
Credentials used for deployment may have access beyond the immediate task.

16
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:57.740
Separate development, staging, and production credentials where possible, use least privilege for automation identities, and clean up workspace material after use.

17
00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.160
The goal is not to distrust automation.

18
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:05.540
The goal is to design automation so secret exposure is narrow and understandable.
